; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Counting in Binary
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Intermediate
; TAGS:     recursion, binary, numbers, animation, lights
; SUMMARY:  Writes every binary number of a given length by recursion, converts numbers to binary, and flashes the count on a row of lights.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   Computers count with only two digits, 0 and 1. Every 3 digit binary
;   number is either a 0 followed by a 2 digit binary number, or a 1
;   followed by one. ALLBITS uses exactly that rule to print the
;   numbers from 000 to 111 in order.
;   TOBINARY goes the other way: the last binary digit of 13 is
;   remainder 13 2, which is 1, and the digits before it are the binary
;   for 13 / 2 (rounded down), which is 6. Then a row of eight lights
;   counts up from 0 to 40 so you can watch the bits change.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run. Read the printout, then watch the lights.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        allbits :n - 1 word :sofar 0
;      Run, and press F5 repeatedly. Watch :sofar in the Variables
;      panel grow one digit at a time, and the Call Stack show how many
;      digits are still to be chosen.
;   3. For TOBINARY, put a breakpoint on its OUTPUT line and look at :n
;      in each call on the Call Stack: 13, 6, 3, 1.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * recursion that builds up an answer in an extra input (:sofar)
;   * the binary place-value system
;   * converting between number bases with QUOTIENT and REMAINDER
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change allbits 3 " to allbits 4 ". How many lines now?
;   * Make ALLBITS count in base 3, using the digits 0, 1 and 2.
;   * Write FROMBINARY that turns "1101 back into 13.
; ============================================================

; :n digits still to choose; :sofar holds the digits chosen so far.
to allbits :n :sofar
  if :n = 0 [print :sofar stop]
  allbits :n - 1 word :sofar 0
  allbits :n - 1 word :sofar 1
end

to tobinary :n
  if :n < 2 [output :n]
  output word tobinary int :n / 2 remainder :n 2
end

; Pad a binary word with zeros on the left until it has :width digits.
to padded :bits :width
  if (count :bits) >= :width [output :bits]
  output padded word 0 :bits :width
end

; Draw the eight lights for :n, with the value of each place written
; above its bulb. A lit bulb is yellow, an unlit one brown.
to lights :n
  localmake "bits padded tobinary :n 8
  localmake "x -280
  localmake "places [128 64 32 16 8 4 2 1]
  foreach :bits [
    bulb :x ifelse ? = 1 [6] [8]
    setpencolor 15
    penup setxy :x - 15 30
    label item # :places
    make "x :x + 80
  ]
  setpencolor 7
  penup setxy -60 -120
  label (sentence :n "= :bits)
end

; A filled circle of radius 30 centered at :x, -30. Walking a circle
; with RT turns from the left edge puts the center to the right.
to bulb :x :color
  penup setxy :x - 30 -30 setheading 0
  setpencolor :color
  pendown
  filled :color [repeat 36 [fd 5.24 rt 10]]
end

print [All the three digit binary numbers:]
allbits 3 "
print []
foreach [5 13 64 100 255] [print (sentence ? [in binary is] tobinary ?)]

clearscreen hideturtle
setbackground [20 20 50]
setlabelheight 24
for [i 0 40] [
  clean
  lights :i
  wait 20
]
